WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Are ordinary people able to do terrible things?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:08.000
And if so, how many would give a strong&nbsp;
electric shock to an innocent other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.480
just because they are following an order?&nbsp;
To answer these questions, we can look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.440
the controversial work of a man who just wanted&nbsp;
to find answers to his family's horrific past.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.800
In 1961, Stanley Milgram, a young&nbsp;
psychologist, wanted to find out how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:35.120
ordinary citizens were able to commit acts of&nbsp;
unspeakable evil in Nazi Germany. His theory:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.960
some people do horrific things because they obey&nbsp;
even the most wicked leaders. To test his theory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.920
Milgram designed a clever experiment that changed&nbsp;
our understanding of human behavior forever.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.640
The Milgram Experiment involved 3 people:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:55.600
An authority, called the experimenter, who&nbsp;
was dressed in a lab coat to appear powerful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:01.040
A volunteer, who was assigned to be the&nbsp;
teacher. And a victim, the so-called student.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.880
The teacher was the test subject, whereas the&nbsp;
experimenter and student were both actors.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.360
Following orders, the teacher should test&nbsp;
a student, who is sitting in another room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.600
by asking them questions. For every&nbsp;
wrong answer the experimenter would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:22.960
ask the teacher to inflict an electric&nbsp;
shock up to a life threatening 450 volts.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.680
Before he began, Milgram asked his colleagues&nbsp;
what they expected the outcome to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:35.120
Almost all of them agreed that only a few of the&nbsp;
volunteers would obey and inflict electric shocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.640
on innocent others. What do you think? Would&nbsp;
anyone administer shocks higher than 300 volts?

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.920
Milgram then advertised his experiment as a&nbsp;
"study on memory and learning" at the campus of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.840
Yale university. People signed up without any idea&nbsp;
of what they were really getting themselves into.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.920
The experiment began with the volunteers meeting&nbsp;
the other participants. The volunteers then pulled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.880
a card to draw their role. Little did they&nbsp;
know that they could only draw the teacher.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.320
Next, they would be given a&nbsp;
sample of a light electric shock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.600
in order to experience firsthand what&nbsp;
the others would have to go through.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:17.680
To start, the experimenter and the&nbsp;
teacher were seated in one room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.880
and the student was strapped&nbsp;
to a chair in an adjacent room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.320
The Teacher and student were able to&nbsp;
communicate, but not see each other.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.800
The experimenter then gave the teacher&nbsp;
a list of questions. The teacher would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.040
then read out the questions and the student&nbsp;
would press a button to indicate a response.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.400
For every false answer, the&nbsp;
teacher would administer a shock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.800
starting at 15 volts and increasing&nbsp;
in 15-volt increments up to 450

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.120
What the teacher did not know was that the&nbsp;
student didn't actually receive any shocks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:54.720
Instead, a tape recorder was used to play various&nbsp;
responses. In the beginning the teacher would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:03:00.960
hear protest, or bangs against the wall. If shocks&nbsp;
would increase the reactions would become louder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.640
And in case someone would go all the&nbsp;
way, the learner would fall silent.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.280
In case the teacher became hesitant and asked&nbsp;
to stop, the experimenter would resort to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.960
following four prompts. First, he would say:&nbsp;
"Please continue". If that was unsuccessful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.400
he would go on with: "The experiment requires&nbsp;
that you continue”. Then: "It is absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:28.240
essential that you continue”. And lastly:&nbsp;
"You have no other choice; you must go on".

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:36.320
Along the way, the volunteers displayed signs&nbsp;
of extreme tension such as sweating, trembling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.520
and even uncontrollable laughing fits. The&nbsp;
experiment would be stopped only after all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:48.080
four prompts had been used or the maximum&nbsp;
voltage of 450 volts had been given three times.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:57.680
Milgram found that of all participants,&nbsp;
100% gave at least 300 volts and 65%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:03.760
went all the way to 450 volts. The experiment was&nbsp;
later criticized for being unethical because&nbsp;it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.720
deceived innocent people into performing&nbsp;
what seem to be terrible acts of violence.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.560
However, his experiment was&nbsp;
successfully replicated many times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.600
involving different populations&nbsp;
and leading to similar findings.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.680
Milgram himself left us with this to think&nbsp;
about: “It may be that we are puppets - puppets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:28.000
controlled by the strings of society. But&nbsp;
at least we are puppets with perception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.880
with awareness. And perhaps our awareness&nbsp;
is the first step to our liberation.”

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.200
What do you think? Would you follow or&nbsp;
question the orders if you were one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.080
Milgram's participants? And what can we as&nbsp;
a society teach future generations to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.120
prevent horrific acts that can happen when&nbsp;
ordinary people blindly follow an authority?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:55.840
Let us know your thoughts in the comments below.

